# FFix intro: one clip, a few useful edits

## What we’ll build

Let’s take one pleasant sample video and use it as a small editing workbench.

We will build up gradually:

* load and preview a source clip
* build the smallest useful `FFix.command/2`
* add filters one at a time
* add centered title text
* save a still image
* make a phone-friendly version
* branch one graph into a few outputs
* observe runner events and progress
* finish with a small visual ffmpeg easter egg

The point is not to hide ffmpeg. The point is to keep ffmpeg concepts visible while making commands and filtergraphs easier to compose from Elixir.

## Setup

This notebook uses:

* `FFix` from GitHub
* `Kino` for inline previews and controls
* `Req` to download a small CC0 sample clip

```elixir
Mix.install(
  [
    {:ffix, github: "akash-akya/ffix"},
    {:kino, "~> 0.19"},
    {:req, "~> 0.5"}
  ],
  consolidate_protocols: false
)
```

```elixir
alias FFix.Filter

unless System.find_executable("ffmpeg") do
  raise "ffmpeg must be available on PATH to run this notebook"
end

work_dir =
  Path.join(System.tmp_dir!(), "ffix-edit-workbench-#{System.unique_integer([:positive])}")

File.mkdir_p!(work_dir)

Kino.Markdown.new("Working in `#{work_dir}`")
```

```elixir
defmodule FFixIntro do
  def run!(command, options \\ []) do
    # Collect full diagnostics for these short tutorial runs.
    FFix.run!(command, Keyword.merge([stderr: :collect], options))
  end

  def video_file(path), do: Kino.Video.new(File.read!(path), :mp4)
  def video_bytes(bytes), do: Kino.Video.new(bytes, :mp4)
  def image_file(path), do: Kino.Image.new(File.read!(path), :jpeg)

  def download(path) do
    Kino.Download.new(fn -> File.read!(path) end, filename: Path.basename(path))
  end

  def command_card(command) do
    fenced("Command FFix will run", FFix.to_shell_string(command))
  end

  def graph_card(%FFix.Command{} = command) do
    # Serialization resolves input declaration identities to labels such as [0:v:0].
    command
    |> FFix.to_argv()
    |> filter_complex_arg()
    |> then(&fenced("Filtergraph generated by FFix", &1))
  end

  def event_table(events) do
    rows =
      Enum.flat_map(events, fn
        {:start, %{argv: argv}} ->
          [%{event: "start", detail: Enum.join(Enum.take(argv, 4), " "), frame: nil, speed: nil}]

        {:progress, progress} ->
          [
            %{
              event: "progress",
              detail: inspect(progress.status),
              frame: progress.frame,
              speed: progress.speed
            }
          ]

        {:log, log} ->
          [%{event: "log", detail: "#{log.level}: #{log.message}", frame: nil, speed: nil}]

        {:exit, result} ->
          [
            %{
              event: "exit",
              detail: "status #{inspect(result.exit_status)}",
              frame: nil,
              speed: nil
            }
          ]

        {:error, error} ->
          [%{event: "error", detail: Exception.message(error), frame: nil, speed: nil}]

        _other ->
          []
      end)

    Kino.DataTable.new(rows)
  end

  def int(value, default, min, max) do
    value = if is_number(value), do: round(value), else: default
    value |> Kernel.max(min) |> Kernel.min(max)
  end

  def text(value, default) do
    value = value |> to_string() |> String.trim()
    if value == "", do: default, else: value
  end

  defp filter_complex_arg(["-filter_complex", graph | _rest]), do: graph
  defp filter_complex_arg([_arg | rest]), do: filter_complex_arg(rest)
  defp filter_complex_arg([]), do: "(no -filter_complex; this command maps streams directly)"

  defp fenced(title, body) do
    fence = String.duplicate("`", 3)

    Kino.Markdown.new("""
    #{title}:

    #{fence}text
    #{body}
    #{fence}
    """)
  end
end
```

## 1. Load the source clip

We use one fixed CC0 sample video so the notebook has a consistent story.

This is an intentional hard dependency for the tutorial: if the download fails, fix the network/sample URL and re-run this cell.

```elixir
source_url = "https://interactive-examples.mdn.mozilla.net/media/cc0-videos/flower.mp4"
source_path = Path.join(work_dir, "source.mp4")

# The tutorial intentionally depends on this small remote MP4.
# One fixed clip keeps every following output easy to compare.
response = Req.get!(source_url, receive_timeout: 15_000)

unless response.status == 200 and is_binary(response.body) do
  raise "expected a downloadable MP4 at #{source_url}, got status #{inspect(response.status)}"
end

File.write!(source_path, response.body)

Kino.Layout.grid([
  Kino.Markdown.new("Downloaded the source clip from `#{source_url}`."),
  FFixIntro.video_file(source_path)
])
```

## 2. First command: trim a tiny preview

Declare the source and output first, then build a command from the output:

```text
source = FFix.input(path, input_options)
video = FFix.video(source)
output = FFix.output(video, target, output_options)
command = FFix.command(output, global: global_options)
```

In this first version there is no filtergraph yet:

* `FFix.input(source_path, t: "2")` declares an ffmpeg input and asks ffmpeg to read only two seconds
* `FFix.video(source)` selects the first video stream (index 0)
* `FFix.output(video, :stdout, ...)` writes a tiny MP4 to stdout, so Livebook can render it without creating a file
* `FFix.command/2` infers inputs from the output's stream dependencies

Configure input options, demuxers, and decoders before selecting streams: selections capture immutable declarations. Reusing a declaration opens it once per command; separate declarations of the same file remain separate inputs. Use separate declarations when you need different decoder or seek settings. Raw CLI switches use `:flag`, while raw booleans emit `1` or `0`.

```elixir
# Input options render before `-i`; this becomes roughly `-t 2 -i source.mp4`.
preview_source = FFix.input(source_path, t: "2")
preview_video = FFix.video(preview_source)

preview_output =
  FFix.output(preview_video, :stdout,
    f: :mp4,
    # MP4 on stdout needs fragmentation; lists serialize as frag_keyframe+empty_moov.
    movflags: [:frag_keyframe, :empty_moov],
    # Quoted atoms/strings work for ffmpeg stream-specific option names.
    "c:v": :libx264,
    # yuv420p is the most broadly playable pixel format for browser previews.
    pix_fmt: :yuv420p,
    an: :flag
  )

preview_cmd = FFix.command(preview_output, global: [y: :flag])

preview = FFixIntro.run!(preview_cmd, stdout: :collect)

Kino.Layout.grid([
  FFixIntro.command_card(preview_cmd),
  FFixIntro.video_bytes(preview.stdout)
])
```

## 3. Add the first filter chain

Now we start doing video work.

Filters compose naturally with the Elixir pipe operator. Here we:

* trim timestamps to start at zero
* normalize frame rate
* resize/crop to 1280×720
* output another tiny MP4 directly to Livebook

```elixir
source = FFix.input(source_path)

normalized_video =
  source
  |> FFix.video()
  # `trim` cuts frames, but it keeps the original timestamps.
  |> Filter.trim(duration: "3")
  # Reset timestamps so the filtered preview starts at t=0.
  |> Filter.setpts(expr: "PTS-STARTPTS")
  |> Filter.fps(fps: 30)
  # Fill a 16:9 canvas without letterboxing, then crop the overflow.
  |> Filter.scale(w: 1280, h: 720, force_original_aspect_ratio: :increase)
  |> Filter.crop(w: 1280, h: 720)

normalized_output =
  FFix.output(normalized_video, :stdout,
    f: :mp4,
    movflags: [:frag_keyframe, :empty_moov],
    "c:v": :libx264,
    pix_fmt: :yuv420p,
    # Encoder speed/quality tradeoff; useful for quick tutorial runs.
    preset: :veryfast,
    an: :flag
  )

normalized_cmd = FFix.command(normalized_output, global: [y: :flag])

normalized = FFixIntro.run!(normalized_cmd, stdout: :collect)

Kino.Layout.grid([
  FFixIntro.graph_card(normalized_cmd),
  FFixIntro.video_bytes(normalized.stdout)
])
```

## 4. Add centered title text

Now we add a title card. Pass ffmpeg expressions such as `(w-tw)/2` as plain strings. FFix escapes them at the filtergraph serialization boundary.

We also use `enable:` to show the title only during part of the clip.

```elixir
title_input = Kino.Input.text("Video title", default: "A tiny garden story")
channel_input = Kino.Input.text("Channel name", default: "FFix Studio")
duration_input = Kino.Input.number("Clip duration", default: 4, min: 2, max: 8, step: 1)

Kino.Layout.grid([title_input, channel_input, duration_input])
```

```elixir
title = FFixIntro.text(Kino.Input.read(title_input), "A tiny garden story")
channel = FFixIntro.text(Kino.Input.read(channel_input), "FFix Studio")
duration_seconds = FFixIntro.int(Kino.Input.read(duration_input), 4, 2, 8)
# Many ffmpeg duration options prefer strings such as "4" or "00:00:04".
duration = Integer.to_string(duration_seconds)
# Leave a little tail without text so the title does not cover the whole clip.
enable_until = max(duration_seconds - 0.4, 1.5)

titled_path = Path.join(work_dir, "01-titled-video.mp4")

titled_video =
  source
  |> FFix.video()
  |> Filter.trim(duration: duration)
  |> Filter.setpts(expr: "PTS-STARTPTS")
  |> Filter.fps(fps: 30)
  |> Filter.scale(w: 1280, h: 720, force_original_aspect_ratio: :increase)
  |> Filter.crop(w: 1280, h: 720)
  |> Filter.drawtext(
    text: title,
    # w/h are frame dimensions; tw/th are rendered text dimensions.
    x: "(w-tw)/2",
    y: "(h-th)/2",
    fontsize: 52,
    fontcolor: "white",
    box: true,
    boxcolor: "black@0.45",
    boxborderw: 18,
    enable: "between(t,0.4,#{enable_until})"
  )

# A quiet placeholder audio track keeps video players happy without adding noise.
titled_audio = Filter.anullsrc(channel_layout: :stereo, sample_rate: 48_000, duration: duration)

titled_output =
  FFix.output([titled_video, titled_audio], titled_path,
    "c:v": :libx264,
    "c:a": :aac,
    pix_fmt: :yuv420p,
    preset: :veryfast,
    # Stop when the generated silent audio or filtered video ends.
    shortest: :flag
  )

titled_cmd = FFix.command(titled_output, global: [y: :flag])

FFixIntro.run!(titled_cmd)

Kino.Layout.grid([
  FFixIntro.graph_card(titled_cmd),
  FFixIntro.video_file(titled_path)
])
```

## 5. Save a still image

A still image is a useful companion to a video. We seek to one second, grab a frame, resize it, and add centered text.

```elixir
thumbnail_title_input = Kino.Input.text("Thumbnail title", default: title)
thumbnail_path = Path.join(work_dir, "02-thumbnail.jpg")

thumbnail_title_input
```

```elixir
thumbnail_title = FFixIntro.text(Kino.Input.read(thumbnail_title_input), title)

# A separate declaration owns the seek; it does not change the other pipelines.
# `ss` before `-i` asks ffmpeg to seek before decoding, which is fast.
thumbnail_source = FFix.input(source_path, ss: "00:00:01")

thumbnail_image =
  thumbnail_source
  |> FFix.video()
  |> Filter.scale(w: 1280, h: 720, force_original_aspect_ratio: :increase)
  |> Filter.crop(w: 1280, h: 720)
  |> Filter.drawtext(
    text: thumbnail_title,
    x: "(w-tw)/2",
    y: "(h-th)/2",
    fontsize: 64,
    fontcolor: "white",
    box: true,
    boxcolor: "black@0.5",
    boxborderw: 24
  )
  |> Filter.drawtext(
    text: channel,
    x: "(w-tw)/2",
    # Place the channel name just below the centered title.
    y: "(h+th)/2+56",
    fontsize: 30,
    fontcolor: "white"
  )

thumbnail_output =
  FFix.output(thumbnail_image, thumbnail_path,
    # One encoded video frame becomes one JPEG.
    "frames:v": 1,
    # Lower q:v is better JPEG quality.
    "q:v": 3,
    f: :image2
  )

thumbnail_cmd = FFix.command(thumbnail_output, global: [y: :flag])

FFixIntro.run!(thumbnail_cmd)

Kino.Layout.grid([
  FFixIntro.graph_card(thumbnail_cmd),
  FFixIntro.image_file(thumbnail_path)
])
```

## 6. Make a phone-friendly version

The same source can become a 9:16 phone-friendly clip. The only big difference is the target canvas: 1080×1920.

```elixir
phone_path = Path.join(work_dir, "03-phone-friendly.mp4")

phone_video =
  source
  |> FFix.video()
  |> Filter.trim(duration: duration)
  |> Filter.setpts(expr: "PTS-STARTPTS")
  |> Filter.fps(fps: 30)
  # Vertical phone canvas. `increase` fills the frame; crop chooses the center.
  |> Filter.scale(w: 1080, h: 1920, force_original_aspect_ratio: :increase)
  |> Filter.crop(w: 1080, h: 1920)
  |> Filter.drawtext(
    text: title,
    x: "(w-tw)/2",
    y: "(h-th)/2",
    fontsize: 72,
    fontcolor: "white",
    box: true,
    boxcolor: "black@0.5",
    boxborderw: 24,
    enable: "between(t,0.4,#{enable_until})"
  )

phone_audio = Filter.anullsrc(channel_layout: :stereo, sample_rate: 48_000, duration: duration)

phone_output =
  FFix.output([phone_video, phone_audio], phone_path,
    "c:v": :libx264,
    "c:a": :aac,
    pix_fmt: :yuv420p,
    preset: :veryfast,
    shortest: :flag
  )

phone_cmd = FFix.command(phone_output, global: [y: :flag])

FFixIntro.run!(phone_cmd)

Kino.Layout.grid([
  FFixIntro.graph_card(phone_cmd),
  FFixIntro.video_file(phone_path)
])
```

## 7. Branch one graph into a few outputs

Now we combine the ideas.

`Filter.split/2` returns a list of branches from one filtered stream. A single-output filter returns one stream reference, and a sink returns a terminal. Each produced pad must be consumed exactly once; use an explicit split for fan-out or a sink for an unused branch. Direct input selections can be reused without a split.

This command writes three outputs in one ffmpeg run:

* 16:9 video
* 9:16 phone-friendly video
* review contact sheet

```elixir
sheet_layout_input =
  Kino.Input.select("Review sheet layout", [{"2x2", "2 × 2"}, {"3x2", "3 × 2"}], default: "2x2")

sheet_layout_input
```

```elixir
sheet_layout = Kino.Input.read(sheet_layout_input)
wide_path = Path.join(work_dir, "04-wide-video.mp4")
phone_branch_path = Path.join(work_dir, "04-phone-friendly.mp4")
sheet_path = Path.join(work_dir, "04-review-sheet.jpg")

# Normalize once before splitting, so each branch starts from the same clean base.
base =
  source
  |> FFix.video()
  |> Filter.trim(duration: duration)
  |> Filter.setpts(expr: "PTS-STARTPTS")
  |> Filter.fps(fps: 30)

[wide, phone, sheet] = Filter.split(base, outputs: 3)

wide_video =
  wide
  |> Filter.scale(w: 1280, h: 720, force_original_aspect_ratio: :increase)
  |> Filter.crop(w: 1280, h: 720)
  |> Filter.drawtext(
    text: title,
    x: "(w-tw)/2",
    y: "(h-th)/2",
    fontsize: 44,
    fontcolor: "white",
    box: true,
    boxcolor: "black@0.45",
    boxborderw: 16,
    enable: "between(t,0.4,#{enable_until})"
  )

phone_branch_video =
  phone
  |> Filter.scale(w: 1080, h: 1920, force_original_aspect_ratio: :increase)
  |> Filter.crop(w: 1080, h: 1920)
  |> Filter.drawtext(
    text: title,
    x: "(w-tw)/2",
    y: "(h-th)/2",
    fontsize: 72,
    fontcolor: "white",
    box: true,
    boxcolor: "black@0.5",
    boxborderw: 24,
    enable: "between(t,0.4,#{enable_until})"
  )

review_sheet =
  sheet
  # Sample one frame per second, then tile those frames into a contact sheet.
  |> Filter.fps(fps: 1)
  |> Filter.scale(w: 180, h: -1)
  |> Filter.tile(layout: sheet_layout, margin: 8, padding: 4, color: "white")

wide_audio = Filter.anullsrc(channel_layout: :stereo, sample_rate: 48_000, duration: duration)

phone_branch_audio =
  Filter.anullsrc(channel_layout: :stereo, sample_rate: 48_000, duration: duration)

wide_output =
  FFix.output([wide_video, wide_audio], wide_path,
    "c:v": :libx264,
    "c:a": :aac,
    pix_fmt: :yuv420p,
    preset: :veryfast,
    shortest: :flag
  )

phone_branch_output =
  FFix.output([phone_branch_video, phone_branch_audio], phone_branch_path,
    "c:v": :libx264,
    "c:a": :aac,
    pix_fmt: :yuv420p,
    preset: :veryfast,
    shortest: :flag
  )

sheet_output =
  FFix.output(review_sheet, sheet_path,
    # The sheet branch is a video stream with one frame; write it as JPEG.
    "frames:v": 1,
    "q:v": 3,
    f: :image2
  )

branch_cmd = FFix.command([wide_output, phone_branch_output, sheet_output], global: [y: :flag])

FFixIntro.run!(branch_cmd)

Kino.Layout.grid([
  FFixIntro.graph_card(branch_cmd),
  FFixIntro.video_file(wide_path),
  FFixIntro.video_file(phone_branch_path),
  FFixIntro.image_file(sheet_path)
])
```

## 8. Observe runner events and progress

`FFix.run!/2` is convenient, but the runner can also stream events.

Here we run the multi-output command again with `progress: true`, then display the important events in a table. The stream is lazy; every enumeration starts a fresh process. Live events are independent of the bounded diagnostics retained in the result. We collect all events only because this is a short clip.

`FFix.run/2` returns operational success/error tuples; `FFix.run!/2` raises `FFix.Runner.Error` for operational failures. Application callbacks and event consumers keep their original exceptions. For Command values, executable selection is `ffmpeg:` first, then `FFMPEG_BIN`, then `ffmpeg` on `PATH`. Raw argv executes literally without executable substitution or extra flags.

```elixir
events =
  branch_cmd
  # `progress: true` adds `-progress pipe:2`; ffmpeg emits machine-readable
  # progress lines, and FFix turns them into `{:progress, %Progress{}}` events.
  |> FFix.stream(progress: true, stderr: {:tail, 65_536})
  |> Enum.to_list()

Kino.Layout.grid([
  FFixIntro.event_table(events),
  FFixIntro.video_file(wide_path),
  FFixIntro.image_file(sheet_path)
])
```

## 9. Tiny easter egg: a weird generated bumper

ffmpeg has wonderful source filters hiding in plain sight. Let’s make a tiny visual bumper from one of them. No input file needed.

```elixir
egg_input =
  Kino.Input.select(
    "Easter egg bumper",
    [
      sierpinski: "Sierpinski",
      mandelbrot: "Mandelbrot",
      life: "Game of Life",
      cellauto: "Rule 110"
    ],
    default: :mandelbrot
  )

egg_input
```

```elixir
bumper_path = Path.join(work_dir, "05-easter-egg-bumper.mp4")

weird_source =
  case Kino.Input.read(egg_input) do
    :sierpinski ->
      # Source filters have no input pads; they synthesize media inside ffmpeg.
      Filter.sierpinski(size: "1280x720", rate: 30, seed: 42, type: :triangle)

    :mandelbrot ->
      # The default easter egg: a generated fractal zoom, still just a video stream.
      Filter.mandelbrot(
        size: "1280x720",
        rate: 30,
        maxiter: 1200,
        start_scale: "3",
        end_scale: "0.25"
      )

    :life ->
      Filter.life(size: "1280x720", rate: 30, random_seed: 42, ratio: "0.22", life_color: "cyan")

    :cellauto ->
      Filter.cellauto(size: "1280x720", rate: 30, rule: 110, random_seed: 42)
  end

bumper_video =
  weird_source
  |> Filter.drawtext(
    text: channel,
    x: "(w-tw)/2",
    y: "(h-th)/2",
    fontsize: 56,
    fontcolor: "white",
    box: true,
    boxcolor: "black@0.5",
    boxborderw: 20
  )

# Add silence; the visual source itself has no audio.
bumper_audio = Filter.anullsrc(channel_layout: :stereo, sample_rate: 48_000, duration: "3")

bumper_output =
  FFix.output([bumper_video, bumper_audio], bumper_path,
    "c:v": :libx264,
    "c:a": :aac,
    pix_fmt: :yuv420p,
    preset: :veryfast,
    shortest: :flag,
    t: 3
  )

bumper_cmd = FFix.command(bumper_output, global: [y: :flag])

FFixIntro.run!(bumper_cmd)

Kino.Layout.grid([
  FFixIntro.graph_card(bumper_cmd),
  FFixIntro.video_file(bumper_path)
])
```

## Wrap-up

You tried a small editing workflow from one source clip:

* previewed a simple command from stdout bytes
* composed a filter chain
* used centered `drawtext` and timeline `enable:`
* saved a still image
* made a phone-friendly version
* branched one graph into multiple outputs
* inspected runner progress events
* generated a playful bumper from ffmpeg source filters

The nice part: each step is ordinary Elixir data until the final execution boundary.

```elixir
Kino.Layout.grid([
  FFixIntro.download(titled_path),
  FFixIntro.download(thumbnail_path),
  FFixIntro.download(phone_path),
  FFixIntro.download(wide_path),
  FFixIntro.download(phone_branch_path),
  FFixIntro.download(sheet_path),
  FFixIntro.download(bumper_path)
])
```
